      "inputTokens": 136,
      "outputTokens": 1029,
      "costUsd": 0.009329,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended here: the integral of two x plus one, all squared. Expanding works. It's four x squared, plus four x, plus one, which integrates term by term to four x cubed over three, plus two x squared, plus x, plus C. But look at this jar of sourdough starter. t hours after it's fed, it rises at three, over the square root of four t plus one, centimetres per hour. Fast at first, then slower and slower. That rate has nothing to expand. So how far does it rise in six hours? And how can algebra, and the chain rule run backwards, find primitives like these?"
    },
