      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 143,
      "outputTokens": 1012,
      "costUsd": 0.0091795,
      "pronunciationText": "The same idea works when the inside isn't linear, as long as the inside's derivative is there. Find the integral of two x, times x squared plus one, cubed. The inside is x squared plus one. Its derivative, two x, is already sitting outside. So guess x squared plus one, to the four, over four. Differentiating gives four times the bracket cubed, times two x, over four. That's two x, times the bracket cubed, exactly the integrand. In general, the integral of u to the n, times d u by d x, is u to the n plus one, over n plus one, plus C, for n not minus one."
    },
