      "outputTokens": 1208,
      "costUsd": 0.010948,
      "pronunciationText": "One more worked example, with a root. Find the integral of x, over the square root of x squared plus four. First, rewrite it as x, times x squared plus four, to the minus a half. The inside is x squared plus four, with derivative two x. We have x, which is half of that, so it's a missing number. Raising the power to a half and dividing by a half doubles it: two times the square root of x squared plus four. Then take half of that. The answer is the square root of x squared plus four, plus C. And x squared plus four is always positive, so this works for every real x. Differentiating back gives x over the root, the integrand exactly."
